WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.720
Workflows don't just pass around text and
numbers. They can also work with real

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.800
documents, PDFs, reports, images, even
video. Files are how your workspace

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.040
holds onto those. Some you upload, others
your workflows can generate. And any block

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.800
can read a file in as a single object or
write a new one back out. If your process

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.100
involves managing content, it'll probably
run through files. Let's see an example.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.800
A lot of the work your team handles starts
as a document, an invoice, a report, notes

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.800
from a call. In Sim, those documents live
in your workspace as files. A file can

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.540
arrive in several ways. You can upload one
in a chat, post one to the API, or pull

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:49.600
one off an email as an attachment. However
it arrived, inside the workspace, every

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.860
document becomes the same thing. A file
object with an ID, a name, a URL, a size,

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.680
and a type. Here's a workflow that
processes them. A Gmail trigger hands its

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.640
attachment to a file block. An agent reads
it, and Supabase stores the result. When

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.340
an email arrives, the trigger starts a
run. It downloads the invoice and makes it

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.520
available as an output that any block can
reference. The file block points at the

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.560
trigger's first attachment. And when the
run reaches it, that reference resolves

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.960
into the invoice itself. The agent reads
the document directly and extracts the

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.860
fields you asked for. The vendor, the
total, and the date. You can see them in

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:29.860
the run's output.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.940
Supabase writes those fields into the
invoices table as a new row. From here,

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.300
they're data that any of your tools can
query. Files go out the same ways they

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.700
come in. A run can email a document,
upload one to drive, or write a new file

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.260
back into the workspace. That was one
document. The trigger fires for every

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.540
email that arrives during the day. And
each invoice is read and written to the

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:01.340
table the same way. After the run, the
document is still in your workspace as a

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.320
file. And the values it carried are rows
in the table. You can go back to either

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.700
one. That's files in Sim. Documents come
in, workflows read them, and the results

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:13.220
land where the rest of your tools can use
them.
